+#### Arrays of arrays
+
+An `[E; N]` that lives in a buffer is one JavaScript array in one place of that buffer, on the stack
+and on the heap alike: `[[E; N]; M]`, a `&[[E; N]]`, a `Box<[E; N]>` and a `Vec<[E; N]>` are all one
+JavaScript array per row, and a pointer to `[E; N]` into one is a plain slot, so the place
+projections (`p.buf[p.off][i]`), the strict offset and the element moves read every one of them the
+same way.
+
+**The heap.** `Box::<[E; N]>::new` and `RawVec` (which keeps its buffer as a `*mut u8` and casts it to
+`*mut [E; N]` on every access) both reach `__rt.unscale_rows`, which types a fresh block as rows
+of the array's size rather than as `E`s (see "Retyping" under "Allocation"). A block holding exactly
+one array keeps that array as its row and also registers the row as a heap block of `E`s, its
+**flat face**: `vec![a, b, c]` is a `Box<[E; 3]>` unsized to a `Box<[E]>` and turned into a
+`Vec<E>`, which frees and grows the row as its own allocation. The block's address, if it was
+already asked (`NonNull::new` asks it), moves to the row with its granule the first time the row is
+used as that allocation (the cast of the block's pointer to its elements, which a `Box` is also
+freed through); a `Vec<[E; N]>` of capacity one never casts that way and frees the block itself,
+granule and all. A fresh block is checked rather than trusted: a
+`Vec<u8>` written through raw pointers is never cast, so it is still fresh, and a fresh block that
+holds anything but what `alloc` or `alloc_zeroed` left in it is sealed as the bytes it holds instead
+of being typed as rows.
+
+**Flattening.** `<[[E; N]]>::as_flattened` builds a `&[E]` of `M * N` elements from the cast of a
+pointer to its first row to `*const E`. `__rt.unwindow` steps into an array that is all its place
+holds (a box of one, a field, a buffer of one array), and answers a slot into a buffer of several
+rows with the same element of the rows' **flattened view** (`__rt._flat_view`): one view per buffer
+of rows, a `Proxy` over an empty array whose element `i` is the place `rows[i / N][i % N]`, so reads
+and writes reach the rows themselves, a replaced row is followed, a buffer that grows is seen at
+its new length, and a write into a row that is not there yet (a `Vec`'s spare capacity) makes it.
+Building one costs nothing per element; reading through one costs a trap per element. Two casts
+never need the view and do not ask for it: the raw unsize `*const [E; N]` to `*const [E]`
+(`__rt.unwindow_slice`, which `vec![..]`'s `Box<[E; N]>` to `Box<[E]>` is) covers exactly one
+array's elements and steps into it, and on the JIR lane a transmute of a *reference* to an array
+(`Arguments::new`'s `&[u8; N]` to `NonNull<u8>`) is `{ buf: array, off: 0 }` inline, a reference to
+an array being the array object. The view of
+a heap block of rows is that block's flat face too, so `Vec<[E; N]>::into_flattened` hands a
+`Vec<E>` an allocation it can grow and free.
+
+The view is the rows' storage rather than a buffer of its own: `addr` answers the rows' address plus
+the offset, so the cast keeps the address and the elements are contiguous; `ptr_eq` and `ptr_cmp`
+take a slot into the view and a slot into a row it holds to be one place, in one order; and the way
+back, `__rt.window` and `__rt.chunk_slice` at the rows' own width from a row boundary, hands the
+rows back, so `as_chunks::<N>()` undoes `as_flattened` and `unscale_rows` climbs from the byte view of
+an array of arrays back to its rows. `as_chunks` of a buffer that is flat at that width still
+aborts (`guard_as_chunks`).
+
+A byte view (`sc`) cast to an array is refused (`__rt.window`): its offset counts bytes of wider
+elements, so an array of bytes over it would read elements where bytes belong. `ptr::swap_nonoverlapping`
+over elements wider than a byte takes exactly that cast, so `swap_with_slice` of such a slice aborts,
+as it did before through `read_array`.
+
+What remains, each a property of the nested representation rather than of the view:
+
+* a raw pointer made from a **reference** to one row (`&rows[i] as *const [E; N]`) is that row's own
+  box, so its address is the row's own rather than the rows' base plus `i` rows, and so is a pointer
+  taken directly to an element of a stack row (`&rows[i][j]`); pointers taken as places
+  (`&raw const rows[i]`, `rows.as_ptr().add(i)`, iteration) and every flattened pointer agree;
+* the strict `offset_from` of two scalar pointers into different rows subtracts offsets within their
+  own buffers;
+* a `Vec<[E; N]>` of capacity one whose elements were cast to (`as_flattened`) after its address was
+  asked keeps that address on its row, so freeing the block gives no granule back: one granule per
+  such vector, which matters only to a loop of about a million of them;
+* in the **default (baseline) lane**, `Vec<[E; N]>::into_flattened` of a vector whose storage
+  `RawVec` allocated (built by `push`, `extend`, `with_capacity`, or grown) reuses the raw byte
