+// The block holding exactly one array -> that array, its row (`unscale_rows`).
+globalThis.__rt._row_of = new WeakMap();
+
+// unscale_rows(p, size, n, mk, es, unscale, kind): `p as *mut [E; n]` where `p`
+// is a byte pointer, `size` the array's size and `es` its element's. This is
+// how an allocation becomes an array of arrays: `Box::<[E; N]>::new`,
+// `Vec<[E; N]>` (`RawVec` keeps a `*mut u8` and casts it back on every access)
+// and `vec![..]`, which is a box of an array of rows.
+//
+// A fresh heap block is retyped to elements of the ARRAY's size, one JavaScript
+// array per row, which is the shape every other array of arrays has: the place
+// projections, the offsets and the element moves then read it as they read
+// `[[E; N]; M]`, `p.buf[p.off][i]`. A block holding exactly one array keeps
+// that array as its row and also registers the row as a heap block of `E`s, its
+// flat face, so a `Box<[E; N]>` that becomes a `Box<[E]>` or a `Vec<E>` (the
+// unsizing `vec![..]` is) frees and grows the row (`unwindow` steps into it).
+// A block already holding rows of this size, and a byte view of one, give the
+// row the pointer names.
+//
+// "Fresh" is checked rather than trusted here. A block of bytes is never cast
+// to anything when it is written as bytes (`*mut u8` is its own element), so a
+// `Vec<u8>` written through raw pointers is still fresh, and retyping it would
+// turn its bytes into rows. A fresh block that holds anything but what `alloc`
+// or `alloc_zeroed` left in it is sealed as the bytes it holds instead.
+//
+// Anything else is a byte pointer reinterpreted as `E`s -- `unscale` is the
+// member that does that, handed over with its scalar `kind`, or nothing when
+// `E` is a byte -- and then a pointer to `E`s cast to an array (`window`). `mk`
+// is the zero factory of `E`; there is none when `E` is a byte, whose zero is 0.
+globalThis.__rt.unscale_rows = (p, size, n, mk, es, unscale, kind) => {
+  if (p === null || typeof p !== "object") return __rt.unscale(p, size, null);
